The Apple Watch Series 12 pairs a smaller display with a wider case than Series 10 and Series 11, even though the pixel resolution hasn't changed, according to Apple's own tech specs.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/apple-watch-series-12.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="899" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1063371" /><br/>
For the 46mm Series 12, Apple's <a href="https://www.apple.com/apple-watch-series-12/specs/">tech specs page</a> lists the display area at 1,196 sq mm, down 2% from 1,220 sq mm on <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/121202">Series 10</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/125093">Series 11</a>. The 42mm size shows a similar 1.9% drop, from 989 sq mm to 970 sq mm. Pixel counts are identical across all three generations: 416 by 496 at 46mm, and 374 by 446 at 42mm.<br/>
<br/>
The case grows as the screen shrinks. Apple's Series 12 tech specs list the 46mm case at 46mm by 40mm by 9.7mm, a millimeter wider than the 46mm by 39mm by 9.7mm case on Series 10 and Series 11, a roughly 2.6% increase in case footprint. The 42mm case grew from 42mm by 36mm to 42mm by 37mm over the same span, about 2.8%, with depth unchanged at 9.7mm for both sizes.<br/>
<br/>
A smaller display combined with a bigger casing means bigger borders around the display, and it looks like Apple has also tweaked the design of the Series 12 to recede the casing and expand the front glass, which makes the borders look even larger.<br/>
<br/>
There's recent precedent for a change like this translating into a visibly thicker bezel. Series 10 went through a similar shift when it replaced Series 9 in 2024, growing the case around the display. The Series 10's visible aluminum edge had actually shrunk by around 33% versus Series 9, while the total bezel, including the black glass border, had grown by about 17%, even though the case and display figures looked like an improvement on paper.<br/>
<br/>
